import Prelude

/-!
# Result

The result monad of Feral's lowered code (RD7), shared by lib/Optic.wiz and
lib/Region.wiz: a value (`ok a`) or a panic (`err e`).

Two readings of a specification over a `Result`: `Result.All` (partial correctness: a
failure satisfies everything) and `Result.Holds` (total correctness: a failure satisfies
nothing). `Result.Holds` is the one that lines up with separation logic's `Triple`,
where a fault (`Prog.fail`) satisfies nothing either.

`ResultQ q A` is the quantity-polymorphic form: `ResultQ ω A` is `Result A`
(definitionally), and `ResultQ 1 A` has a linear success value. `Result1` (lib/Region.wiz)
is its resource-threading sibling `ResultH 1`, not an instance of `ResultQ`: its error
branch carries a handle. See `wiz-notes/result-q.md`.
-/

-- The result monad of lowered code (RD7): every panic (`panic!`, `unwrap` on `none`,
-- overflow, an index out of bounds, division by zero) is an `err`.
/-- Why lowered code failed (RD7): every panic (`panic!`, `unwrap` on `none`, overflow, an
index out of bounds, division by zero) is an `Error`, carried by `Result.err`. -/
inductive Error where
  | panic (msg : String)
  | overflow
  | outOfBounds
  | unwrapNone
  | divZero

/-- The result monad of lowered code: a value (`ok a`) or a panic (`err e`). -/
inductive Result (A : Type) where
  | ok (a : A)
  | err (e : Error)

/-- Sequence two results, stopping at the first error. Lowered code matches on results
directly (no closures around owners); `bind` and the monad instance are for
hand-written specs and Wiz code calling lowered code (RD16). -/
def Result.bind {A B : Type} (x : Result A) (f : A → Result B) : Result B := match x with
  | .ok a => f a
  | .err e => .err e
/-- Apply `f` to the value, if there is one. -/
def Result.map {A B : Type} (f : A → B) (x : Result A) : Result B := match x with
  | .ok a => .ok (f a)
  | .err e => .err e
/-- `Result` is a monad, so it has `do` notation. -/
instance : Monad Result where
  pure := fun a => Result.ok a
  bind := Result.bind

-- ## Quantity-polymorphic results (`ResultQ`)

/-- A result whose success value is used at quantity `q`: `ok` carries the value at `%q`,
`err` carries only the `Error` (no payload). At `q = ω` this is exactly `Result`
(definitionally; `Result.toResultQ`/`ResultQ.toResult` are the identity), and at `q = 1`
the success value is linear. It is **not** `Result1`: an `err` has no value, so a linear
resource cannot be threaded through the error path (see `ResultH` in lib/Region.wiz,
whose `err` carries a handle).

`q = 0`: the value is at quantity 0, so it may not be used by a consumer (`ResultQ.elim`
takes it at `%0` and the branch may ignore it); constructing `.ok a` needs `a` at 0.
The generic compiled representation still keeps the field (erasure is compiled once at a
quantity variable), as for any q-polymorphic type: `wiz-notes/result-q.md` §3. -/
inductive ResultQ (q : Qty) (A : Type) where
  | ok (%q a : A)
  | err (e : Error)

/-- `Result ω` as a `ResultQ ω` (the identity: the two types are definitionally equal). -/
def Result.toResultQ {A : Type} (x : Result A) : ResultQ ω A := x
/-- `ResultQ ω` as a `Result` (the identity). -/
def ResultQ.toResult {A : Type} (x : ResultQ ω A) : Result A := x

/-- Sequence two results, stopping at the first error; the success value goes to `f` at
the input's quantity `q`, and the result is at `p`. The scrutinee is consumed once, so a
linear success value cannot be duplicated; `f` itself is unrestricted because the `err`
branch drops it. -/
def ResultQ.bind {q p : Qty} {A B : Type} (1 x : ResultQ q A)
    (f : (%q a : A) → ResultQ p B) : ResultQ p B := match x with
  | .ok a => f a
  | .err e => .err e
/-- Apply `f` to the success value, at the input's quantity `q`. -/
def ResultQ.map {q : Qty} {A B : Type} (f : (%q a : A) → B) (x : ResultQ q A) : ResultQ q B :=
  match x with
  | .ok a => .ok (f a)
  | .err e => .err e
/-- Case analysis: the success branch takes the value at `%q`, the error branch takes the
`Error`. Both branches are unrestricted (each is dropped on the other path). -/
def ResultQ.elim {q : Qty} {A B : Type} (onOk : (%q a : A) → B) (onErr : (e : Error) → B)
    (1 x : ResultQ q A) : B := match x with
  | .ok a => onOk a
  | .err e => onErr e

/-- `P` holds of the value, if there is one (a failure satisfies everything): "if it
succeeds, then `P`". -/
def Result.All {A : Type} (P : A → Prop) : Result A → Prop
  | .ok a => P a
  | .err _ => True
/-- There is a value, and `P` holds of it (an `∃` without `∃`): "it succeeds, and `P`". -/
def Result.Holds {A : Type} (P : A → Prop) : Result A → Prop
  | .ok a => P a
  | .err _ => False
